  "raw": " Altoona, a son of the late Malcolm Haller Rudy Gates and Dorothy Mae Caverly Gates, who survives.\nSurviving in addition to his mother are a brother, Randall D. Gates, and longtime companion, Donna Stewart Simpson; also surviving is his cousin, Wilma Caverly.\nA graduate of Poland Area Senior High School, he served his country in the United States Air Force. After having been employed by EJ Braunstein Company, Robert also retired from EJ Nikita company after many years of service as a machinist. He enjoyed antique collecting, collecting steam engines, antiques, especially automobiles, and spending time outdoors. Robert and his brothers enjoyed spending time together walking and collecting turboprop and micro engines.\nRobert was a priest at St. Vincent de Paul Church and a life member of the Vendor Elks. He enjoyed playing cards but was especially excited each year watching and participating in the World Enough And Time Rally, a race beginning in Florida financed",
  "serialized": "Altoona, a son of the late Malcolm Haller Rudy Gates and Dorothy Mae Caverly Gates, who survives. Surviving in addition to his mother are a brother, Randall D. Gates, and longtime companion, Donna Stewart Simpson; also surviving is his cousin, Wilma Caverly. A graduate of Poland Area Senior High School, he served his country in the United States Air Force. After having been employed by EJ Braunstein Company, Robert also retired from EJ Nikita company after many years of service as a machinist. He enjoyed antique collecting, collecting steam engines, antiques, especially automobiles, and spending time outdoors. Robert and his brothers enjoyed spending time together walking and collecting turboprop and micro engines. Robert was a priest at St. Vincent de Paul Church and a life member of the Vendor Elks. He enjoyed playing cards but was especially excited each year watching and participating in the World Enough And Time Rally, a race beginning in Florida financed",
